```

This code tests the contract of `PUT_SetContract` method. It assumes an instance of `ArrayList` and an index. It makes a copy of the original count of the `ArrayList`, original index of the element in `ArrayList`, and whether this element is contained in the `ArrayList`. It then replaces an element at the requested index with a different value. The count in the `ArrayList` is then checked to verify that it has increased, and the index of the new element is checked. It also checks whether the old array still contains that element.

The method under test (the `PUT_SetContract`) replaces elements in an `ArrayList`, so it has to be implemented as follows:

```CSharp
using System.Collections;

public void PUT_SetContract(ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
{
    arrList[index] = x;
}
```

Here, `arrList[index] = x;` replaces the element at index with new value x.

## Test-driven development with Pex

Test-driven development (TDD) involves writing the tests first and then writing the code that passes the tests. Using Pex, you can write test code first with the expected behavior, and Pex generates the implementation.

### Step-by-step guide

1. Use Pex to generate tests:

```Csharp
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Validation;
using System.Collections;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [PexClass(typeof(ArrayList))]
    public partial class ArrayListTest
    {
        ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_AddInt32(int x)
        {
            arrList.Add(x);
            Assume.True(arrList.Contains(x));
            arrList.Remove(x);
            Assume.False(arrList.Contains(x));
        }
    }
}
```

2. Run the test. Pex finds a failing test case, such as when trying to add two items with the same value.
3. Implement the fix:

```Csharp
public void PUT_AddInt32(int x)
{
    arrList.Add(x);
    Console.WriteLine(arrList.Count);
    Assume.True(arrList.Contains(x));
    arrList.Remove(x);
    Assume.False(arrList.Contains(x));
}
```
 
4. Re-run the tests. Now they pass.
5. Refactor your code as you would with other TDD.

## Conclusion

Pex, the Visual Studio Extension, can help you catch edge-case bugs before your code ever goes to production. The extension helps you create better software by guiding you to the most interesting inputs for a method or property. It's a crucial tool for developers.

Please refer to [Microsoft’s Visual Studio Test Tools](https://visualstudio.microsoft.com/vs/features/testing-tools/) to know more about other testing tools available for your needs. 

You can explore more about Pex on the official [GitHub Repository](https://github.com/microsoft/Pex)

