A: Your CSS should look like:
.container {
  background: url('http://example.com/image.jpg') no-repeat center center fixed; 
  -webkit-background-size: cover;
  -moz-background-size: cover;
  -o-background-size: cover;
  background-size: cover;
}

You are adding the image directly in the CSS of your container class. The URL of the image is specified with the background property. The no-repeat, center and fixed are properties that make the image not repeat itself and to be centered and fixed once.
The webkit, moz, o and o-background-size properties help to ensure the background image covers the whole area of the element.

A: You can simply set the background image using the following code in your CSS: 
.container {
   background-image: url("http://example.com/image.jpg");
}

If you want the image to cover the full width/height of the .container element, then using: 
.container {
   background-image: url("http://example.com/image.jpg");
   background-size: cover;
}

Last but not least, in order for the children elements to appear against the background, you should also set the color property to a non-transparent color. For example: 
.container {
   background-image: url("http://example.com/image.jpg");
   background-size: cover;
   color: #FFF; 
}


