A: Assuming that you've decorated your ArrayList with the appropriate members to permit Pex to fathom it:
[PexMethod]
public void PUT( [PexAssumeNotNull] ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
{
    Assume.True(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

    int oldVal = (int)arrList[index];
    arrList[index] = x;

    int newVal = (int)arrList[index];

    arrList[index] = oldVal; 
    // Ensure that the next test case doesn't interfere with this one
}

This code will find "interesting" combinations of parameters to test. The 'Assume.True' is used to ensure that the index is within the bounds of the list. The cast to int is necessary because the ArrayList.Items are of type object.
This code does not directly verify whether the insertion into the ArrayList changes any other part of the ArrayList’s state. To perform a comprehensive verification of the ArrayList’s behavior, you would need a more complex fuzzing/symbolic execution strategy.
NOTE: The code above does not check whether the 'index' is outside the valid range of values that the 'arrList' can hold. Check if 'index' is in range before setting it.
Remember that the 'PexAttribute' requires an assembly reference to the Microsoft Fakes Framework, if not installed, you may download it from the Microsoft website and add it to your references.
Please, let me know if you need more support on this topic.

A: The property you're after can be tested via the following 'Pex' method,
[PexAssembly]
[PexMethod(Action = PexMethodAction.None, StateBehavior = StateBehavior.UnderTest)]
public void PUT([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList arrList, int oldValue, int newValue, int index)
{
    arrList[index] = newValue;

    Assert.AreEqual(newValue, arrList[index]);
}

In this testing, Pex will try to find combinations of oldValue, newValue and index that verify the expected behavior of the ArrayList's behavior upon assigning a new value to a given index.
Please replace the type in the test with the actual one you're dealing with (e.g., replace int with the actual type you're using).
Pex's role: Pex will not generate invalid or out of range parameters.


*

*The 'OldValue' is a value in the 'arrList' that is replaced.

*The 'NewValue' is the value that replaces the old value.

*The 'Index' is the location in the list for the 'arrList' where the new value would be inserted. It should be in the range [0, arrList.Count)
The 'arrList' is the list on which testing occurs, and it is assumed that it has been validated as not null because we're under a test.
This code assumes you've set up your unit test to use Microsoft Fakes to handle the state. 
If you're not familiar with Microsoft Fakes, it provides a new abstraction for testing code that uses static and shared members. It's available as a Visual Studio 2012/2013 extension. 

Please ensure Microsoft Fakes reference is added in the test project settings (Add reference -> Extensions -> Microsoft Fakes xx.xx.xx). 

Also ensure your test project is targetting the .Net 4.5, as the Pex and Microsoft Fakes are available only for .Net 4.5 .
If you face these restrictions, you'll need to revise your approach. You could write separate unit tests for inserting at both beginning and end of the list, for example, and Pex will find more interesting combinations for you.
Hope it assists you in achieving your purpose.
Cheers,
Ravishankar

